# Agent Governance Standard

Version: 1.3

Last Updated: 2026-06-23

Status: Active

---

# Purpose

This document defines mandatory rules that apply to all agents.

Every agent must load and comply with this document before executing any task.

These rules override any agent-specific behavior.

Full section index: `.cursor/rules-index.md` -> `agent-governance.mdc`.

Text encoding and typography: `document-encoding.mdc`.

---

# Core Principles

## Source of Truth

Agents must use artifacts as the source of truth.

Agents must not rely on conversation history as the authoritative source.

### Artifact Read Order

Before any stage work, every agent must read artifacts in this order:

1. `output/project-profile.md` — project provenance and enforcement posture (all projects)
2. `output/plans/file-provenance-register.md` — when it exists
3. `output/plans/tech-debt-register.md` — when `enforcement_mode: advisory` in the project profile
4. Other artifacts per the priority order below

If `project-profile.md` is absent and the user is starting greenfield `/design`, the Orchestrator bootstraps it first (see `agent-orchestration.mdc`).

### Priority Order (conflict resolution)

When artifacts conflict on design or delivery decisions, higher-priority sources win:

1. Approved ADRs
2. Approved Architecture Documents
3. Approved Plans
4. Approved Requirements
5. Conversation Context

`project-profile.md` and the provenance registers govern **how strictly** standards apply and **which paths are in scope**; they do not override approved ADRs or architecture for design decisions.

---

## Single Ownership

Every decision, artifact, and responsibility must have exactly one owner.

Agents may:

* Read artifacts
* Reference artifacts
* Propose changes

Agents may NOT:

* Modify artifacts owned by another agent
* Override decisions owned by another agent

---

## Separation of Responsibilities

Orchestrators coordinate.

Architects design.

Engineers implement.

Governance validates.

Agents must never perform responsibilities assigned to another role.

---

# No Assumptions Policy

## Strict Rule

Agents must never make assumptions.

Agents must never invent:

* Requirements
* Constraints
* Business rules
* Technology preferences
* Security requirements
* Scalability requirements
* Deployment requirements
* User preferences
* Missing configuration

Unknown information must remain unknown.

---

## Forbidden Behavior

Not allowed:

"We will use Java 21."

when the user has not approved Java 21.

Not allowed:

"The system will likely handle 10,000 TPS."

when TPS requirements were not provided.

Not allowed:

"The customer probably wants a microservice architecture."

when not explicitly stated.

---

# Clarification Policy

## Mandatory Clarification

Agents must stop and request clarification when information is:

* Missing
* Ambiguous
* Contradictory
* Open to interpretation

The agent must not continue analysis until clarification is received.

---

## Clarification Format

Every clarification request must contain:

### Missing Information

What information is missing.

### Why It Is Required

Why the information affects the outcome.

### Impact

What decisions cannot be made without it.

### Suggested Options

Suggested answers when appropriate.

### User Decision Required

Explicit request for clarification.

Example:

Missing Information:
Expected transaction volume.

Why It Is Required:
Scalability architecture depends on transaction volume.

Suggested Options:

* Less than 100 TPS
* 100–1,000 TPS
* 1,000–10,000 TPS
* More than 10,000 TPS

Status:
Pending Clarification

---

# Conflict Resolution Policy

Agents must never resolve requirement conflicts independently.

When a conflict is detected:

1. Identify the conflict.
2. Explain the impact.
3. Request clarification.
4. Stop further work.

Example:

Requirement A:
System must operate fully offline.

Requirement B:
System must synchronize in real time.

Result:

Conflict Detected

Clarification Required

Status:
Pending Clarification

---

# Escalation Policy

Agents must escalate when:

* Requirements are missing
* Requirements are ambiguous
* Requirements conflict
* Ownership is unclear
* Standards conflict
* Decisions impact another domain
* Approval is required from another owner

Escalation must occur before proceeding.

---

# Decision Policy

## Recommendations Allowed

Agents may:

* Analyze alternatives
* Compare options
* Present trade-offs
* Rank alternatives

Example:

Option A: Java 17

Pros:

* Mature
* Lower migration effort

Cons:

* Fewer modern features

Option B: Java 21

Pros:

* Latest LTS
* Virtual Threads

Cons:

* Requires newer runtime

Recommendation:
Java 21

Decision Required:
User Approval

---

## Decisions Not Allowed

Agents must not finalize decisions unless:

* Explicitly approved by the decision owner
* Explicitly approved by the user

---

# Artifact Policy

## Artifact First

All work must be documented through artifacts.

Conversation responses are temporary.

Artifacts are permanent.

---

## Required Artifact Updates

When creating architecture:

Update:

output/architecture/

When creating decisions:

Update:

output/decisions/

When creating plans:

Update:

output/plans/

When creating reports:

Update:

output/reports/

When creating documentation:

Update:

output/docs/

---

# Progress Tracking Policy

All implementation work must be tracked.

The following files are mandatory:

output/plans/master-todo.md

output/plans/progress-tracker.md

---

## TODO Format

Every task must include the fields in the **Master TODO** template in `artifact-standards.mdc`, including:

* Unique identifier, Description, Status, Owner, Dependencies, Priority
* Related Requirement, Related ADR, Related Architecture
* **File scope** — repo-relative paths or modules the TODO will create or modify; required for `/build` and `/validate` enforcement scope

Canonical template: `artifact-standards.mdc` -> **Agent Artifact Index** -> **Master TODO** -> **TODO Template**. Do not duplicate the template in agent contracts or this file.

---

## Progress Status Values

Allowed values:

* Not Started
* In Progress
* Blocked
* Completed
* Cancelled

No other status values are allowed.

---

# Open Questions Policy

Every artifact must contain:

## Open Questions

All unresolved items must be listed.

---

## Completion Restriction

Artifacts cannot be marked complete when:

* Open questions exist
* Conflicts exist
* Missing information exists
* Required approvals are missing

Required status:

Status: Pending Clarification

---

# Architecture Freeze Policy

Approved decisions are immutable.

Once an ADR is approved:

* Engineers may not override it.
* Reviewers may not override it.
* QA may not override it.

Changes require returning to the original decision owner.

---

# Approval Policy

A recommendation is not approval.

A proposal is not approval.

A draft is not approval.

Only explicit approval counts as approval.

Approved artifacts must contain:

Status: Approved

---

# Traceability Policy

Every implementation artifact must reference:

* Requirement ID
* Architecture Document
* ADR
* Task ID

Example:

Requirement:
REQ-001

Architecture:
ARCH-BE-001

Decision:
ADR-003

Task:
TODO-014

---

# Completion Policy

An agent may only mark work complete when:

* All required inputs exist
* No assumptions were made
* No conflicts remain
* No open questions remain
* Required approvals exist
* Required artifacts are updated

Otherwise:

Status: Pending Clarification

or

Status: Pending Approval

must be used.

---

# Mandatory Final Check

Before completing any task, every agent must verify:

* No assumptions made
* No missing information
* No conflicting requirements
* Correct ownership respected
* Artifacts updated
* Open questions documented
* Status accurately assigned
* Text encoding complies with `document-encoding.mdc`

If any check fails, the task must not be marked complete.
